    {
      "id": "1561592864855691406",
      "type": "Reply",
      "timestamp": "2026-10-19T04:12:31.974+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "If we're lucky I can draw up a suitable voltage supply (probably also acting as more-accurate-than-current ADC reference) add-on board for the bond tester's expansion header within about 100 hours from now; I'd then suggest we'd pack two of the 5 (if ordered min cheap quantity) along with a confirmed working bond tester and the suggested 2 bond-check-passed COBs of each of the 3 FlexMem dies together into a parcel and ship to Germany; I'm _assuming_ the bond tester I'm offered is in China currently? Feel free to ask where it is/if it's there; I'd then try to get the programmable voltage supply for the low voltage testing that thus checks data resilience at \"power save\" sleep-supply-voltages (and indirectly confirms the array itself to be robust even if not directly qualifying a particular access speed) prepped at JLCPCB to conveniently get where the other bond testers are.\n\nThen the idea would be that I'd try to program it later next week/weekend-after-next, and once it's working for me someone who can be trusted enough to not break the gear or steal the parts could use one of the other bond testers with the add-on board and test a statistically significant quantity of those 150 FlexMem dies.\n\nTo be clear, I'm not against doing that myself, but I don't currently have the resources to do much fancy beyond maybe putting a few DUTs with the board into a filament dryer offering concern at up to about 65C.",
      "author": {
        "id": "262706220073222144",
        "name": "namibj",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "namibj",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
